1
00:00:00,670 --> 00:00:05,620
26 April 1986. Pripyat, Soviet Ukraine.

2
00:00:05,750 --> 00:00:10,260
Nearby, at the Chernobyl nuclear
power plant, a test was under way.

3
00:00:11,670 --> 00:00:14,080
1:23 at night.

4
00:00:16,830 --> 00:00:21,320
The reactor had exploded. The core
was burning under the open sky.

5
00:00:21,430 --> 00:00:26,580
Firefighters arrived within minutes. They
did not know what they were fighting.

6
00:00:26,690 --> 00:00:32,160
No protective clothing, no knowledge of
radiation. Only the fire in front of them.

7
00:00:32,270 --> 00:00:35,280
Many of them died within a few weeks.

8
00:00:35,390 --> 00:00:38,400
And Pripyat was still sleeping peacefully.

9
00:00:38,510 --> 00:00:42,960
This Ferris wheel was due to
open on 1 May. It never ran.

10
00:00:46,670 --> 00:00:50,760
Morning came. On the horizon,
a column of smoke was rising.

11
00:00:51,330 --> 00:00:53,820
But nobody was told anything.

12
00:00:53,930 --> 00:00:59,280
It was a Saturday. Children went to school,
weddings took place. Life went on as before.

13
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:04,650
Meanwhile, the reactor burned. Helicopters
began dropping sand and lead into it.

14
00:01:05,170 --> 00:01:11,300
Thirty-six hours later, the loudspeakers
announced it: the whole city must be evacuated.

15
00:01:12,670 --> 00:01:15,920
Outside the city, a long line
of buses was already waiting.

16
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:20,490
People were told to take only
essentials and their documents.

17
00:01:20,600 --> 00:01:23,590
Fifty thousand people.
Three and a half hours.

18
00:01:23,700 --> 00:01:26,570
They thought it would
only be for a few days.

19
00:01:26,680 --> 00:01:30,430
By that evening, Pripyat
was completely empty.

20
00:01:37,080 --> 00:01:43,690
For the next two weeks, helicopters kept dropping sand,
lead and boron into the reactor. Five thousand tonnes.

21
00:01:45,420 --> 00:01:51,390
The machines that fought this battle became so
radioactive they had to be abandoned where they stood.

22
00:01:53,330 --> 00:01:59,180
In September, debris had to be cleared from the
roof of Unit 3. Machines could not work there.

23
00:01:59,290 --> 00:02:04,000
So people were sent. Each
man had just ninety seconds.

24
00:02:04,170 --> 00:02:10,620
By November, a concrete tomb stood over
the broken reactor. The sarcophagus.

25
00:02:12,920 --> 00:02:19,010
About six hundred thousand people helped contain
the disaster. They were called the liquidators.

26
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:22,530
And Pripyat has stood empty ever since.

27
00:02:27,080 --> 00:02:30,090
Until now, the world
had been told nothing.

28
00:02:30,200 --> 00:02:35,570
On the night of 28 April, Soviet television
gave the news just twenty seconds.

29
00:02:35,830 --> 00:02:40,720
On 1 May, in Kyiv, the May Day
parade went ahead as usual.

30
00:02:40,830 --> 00:02:43,980
People did not know there
was radiation in the air.

31
00:02:45,830 --> 00:02:53,080
Beneath the reactor, water had flooded in. If the molten core
had reached it, there could have been a second explosion.

32
00:02:53,330 --> 00:02:57,100
Three men went down into the
dark water and opened the valves.

33
00:02:57,210 --> 00:03:01,940
From the 30-kilometre zone, one hundred
and sixteen thousand people were evacuated.

34
00:03:05,830 --> 00:03:09,300
On 10 May, the fire in the
core was finally put out.

35
00:03:11,670 --> 00:03:16,120
But by then, the radioactive
cloud had spread across Europe.

36
00:03:21,250 --> 00:03:25,520
The pine forest near the plant turned
red from the radiation and died.

37
00:03:26,250 --> 00:03:29,680
It was bulldozed and buried in the ground.

38
00:03:30,830 --> 00:03:35,420
In Pripyat's schools, books
still lie open on the desks.

39
00:03:40,420 --> 00:03:46,670
In October 1986, right beside the ruined
reactor, Unit 1 was started up again.

40
00:03:50,420 --> 00:03:57,230
In July 1987, a trial was held. Three of the
plant's officials were sentenced to ten years.

41
00:04:00,830 --> 00:04:03,680
Pripyat's lights never came on again.

42
00:04:11,250 --> 00:04:16,300
Some elderly people ignored the government.
They came back to their villages.

43
00:04:17,500 --> 00:04:20,010
For them, this land was home.

44
00:04:23,330 --> 00:04:28,960
In the 1990s, thyroid cancer began to spread
among those who had been children in '86.

45
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:34,750
Radioactive iodine had reached
their bodies through milk.

46
00:04:35,830 --> 00:04:41,320
11 October 1991. Fire broke out
in the turbine hall of Unit 2.

47
00:04:42,500 --> 00:04:44,790
That unit never ran again.

48
00:04:48,330 --> 00:04:54,700
In December, the Soviet Union came to an end. The zone
was now the responsibility of an independent Ukraine.

49
00:05:01,250 --> 00:05:07,260
Checkpoints were set up around the zone. Two
thousand six hundred square kilometres, closed off.

50
00:05:08,330 --> 00:05:11,420
Only people with
permission could go inside.

51
00:05:13,330 --> 00:05:18,100
In the 1990s, looters stripped
Pripyat almost completely.

52
00:05:19,170 --> 00:05:25,200
Furniture, metal, even radiators.
Much of it was radioactive.

53
00:05:25,830 --> 00:05:29,500
And slowly, the forest
began to take the city back.

54
00:05:31,250 --> 00:05:34,960
The same street, the same
bicycle. Now among the trees.

55
00:05:38,330 --> 00:05:45,200
In the forest stood a secret Soviet radar
too. Duga. Now it also stands silent.

56
00:05:51,250 --> 00:05:54,720
The hastily built sarcophagus
was starting to fall apart.

57
00:05:55,830 --> 00:06:00,460
Its walls had cracks, and rainwater
leaked in through the roof.

58
00:06:03,750 --> 00:06:08,940
The other reactors were shut
down one by one. Unit 1 in '96.

59
00:06:15,830 --> 00:06:20,700
15 December 2000. 1:17 in the afternoon.

60
00:06:21,250 --> 00:06:25,340
The last reactor, Unit 3,
was shut down for good.

61
00:06:29,170 --> 00:06:32,360
After twenty-three years, the
Chernobyl plant fell silent.

62
00:06:41,250 --> 00:06:44,380
Where people left, nature returned.

63
00:06:45,830 --> 00:06:51,940
In 1998, rare wild horses were released
here. Their herd is still growing today.

64
00:06:54,170 --> 00:06:57,980
Wolves, elk, deer, wild boar.

65
00:06:59,170 --> 00:07:04,320
Without people, their numbers grew even
larger than in the surrounding forests.

66
00:07:06,670 --> 00:07:10,500
Pripyat was slowly
disappearing into the forest.

67
00:07:18,330 --> 00:07:24,700
The radiation is still there. But the absence
of people was a greater relief for the animals.

68
00:07:31,250 --> 00:07:34,400
The old sarcophagus could
not last much longer.

69
00:07:35,830 --> 00:07:41,360
Many countries came together to
build a giant steel arch beside it.

70
00:07:43,330 --> 00:07:47,280
Two hundred and fifty-seven metres
wide, one hundred and eight metres high.

71
00:07:49,170 --> 00:07:52,700
The largest structure ever moved on land.

72
00:07:55,830 --> 00:08:01,580
In November 2016, it was slid
along rails over the old reactor.

73
00:08:08,330 --> 00:08:13,020
For a hundred years, this arch will
protect the world from this reactor.

74
00:08:21,250 --> 00:08:23,960
But people paid the price for all of this.

75
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:30,190
Firefighters, operators, liquidators.
The ones who saved the world.

76
00:08:33,330 --> 00:08:40,080
In the first few weeks alone, thirty-one people
died. They were buried in sealed coffins in Moscow.

77
00:08:45,830 --> 00:08:53,020
About three hundred and fifty thousand people had to leave
their homes forever. Hundreds of villages vanished from the map.

78
00:08:58,330 --> 00:09:01,560
How many lives were lost in
total is still debated today.

79
00:09:03,330 --> 00:09:06,300
From four thousand to
more than sixty thousand.

80
00:09:11,250 --> 00:09:15,320
In 2011, the zone was opened to tourists.

81
00:09:16,670 --> 00:09:20,620
People come to see the Ferris
wheel that never turned.

82
00:09:23,330 --> 00:09:28,700
The Azure swimming pool. Liquidators kept
swimming here for ten years after the disaster.

83
00:09:35,830 --> 00:09:41,360
Beneath the reactor, there is still a
mass of melted fuel. The Elephant's Foot.

84
00:09:43,330 --> 00:09:47,420
Once, standing near it for just
a few minutes could be fatal.

85
00:09:48,330 --> 00:09:52,840
In 2019, one hundred and twenty-four
thousand people came here.

86
00:10:01,330 --> 00:10:05,460
Today, a giant steel arch
stands over Chernobyl.

87
00:10:06,170 --> 00:10:10,260
Beneath it lies the reactor
that changed the world.

88
00:10:13,670 --> 00:10:17,000
Pripyat. Once home to
forty-nine thousand people.

89
00:10:18,330 --> 00:10:21,640
Today there is only
forest here, and silence.

90
00:10:25,500 --> 00:10:29,810
Parts of this land will remain
radioactive for thousands of years.

91
00:10:30,170 --> 00:10:34,700
This story is in memory of
everyone who paid the price.
